import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The email package: installing the plugin, sending a message from a job, several recipients and
## the rest of a message, the SMTP server's URL, the dev mailbox, and what it can't do yet. Static:
## the samples are server/test/app's, which the server's tests run.
meta = {
	title: "Email",
	description: "packages/email, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: email over SMTP, as text or text and HTML, to several recipients with Cc, Bcc, Reply-To and headers of your own, sent from a job so a failed send is tried again; with no SMTP server, kept in memory and shown in the dev mailbox.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "send", label: "Sending an email" },
		{ id: "recipients", label: "Several recipients, Cc, Bcc, Reply-To" },
		{ id: "smtp", label: "Connecting to an SMTP server" },
		{ id: "mailbox", label: "The dev mailbox" },
		{ id: "inside", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · email"
				title="Email that goes out over SMTP, or waits in a mailbox while you develop"
			>
				A welcome, a sign-in link, a receipt: <code>Email.send!</code> hands a message to your SMTP server,
				as text or as text and HTML. While you develop, with no SMTP server set, the message is kept in the
				server's memory and shown in the dev mailbox. Nothing reaches a real inbox by accident, and nothing
				is lost: a deployed server with no SMTP server doesn't start.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package runs on the server only. Its <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem}#plugins"}>plugin</a> adds the
				dev mailbox's pages and checks your SMTP server at start; you send with the <code>Email</code>
				module.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header (<code>{"email: \"../packages/email/main.roc\""}</code>), import
				<code>email.Email</code>, and list its plugin in your program's <code>plugins</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.email_plugin.file}, in part"} source={Sources.email_plugin.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>Both options have defaults:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>from : Str</code></td><td><code>MAIL_FROM</code> from the environment, or else <code>{"rocx <rocx@localhost>"}</code></td><td>who email is from: <code>{"\"Acme <hello@example.com>\""}</code>. <code>MAIL_FROM</code>, if set, wins, so a deploy names the sender.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"smtp_url! : {} => Str"}</code></td><td>reads <code>SMTP_URL</code> from the environment</td><td>where email goes, read once at start. Give your own function to read it from elsewhere, such as a secrets vault. <code>""</code> means no SMTP server, which only <code>--dev</code> (and <code>--debug</code>, for tests) allows.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="send">Sending an email</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Email.send!({ to, subject, text, html })"}</code> sends one message and waits for the SMTP
				server's answer. <code>to</code> is an address, with or without a name
				(<code>{"\"Ada Lovelace <ada@example.com>\""}</code>), or several (below). Leave out
				<code>html</code>, or give <code>""</code>, for text only. It answers <code>{"Ok({})"}</code>, or
				<code>EmailErr</code> with the reason: an address it can't read, a header it refuses, a server that
				refuses the message or can't be reached, or no SMTP server outside <code>--dev</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Send from a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a>, not from the request: the request doesn't wait for
				the SMTP server, and a failed send is tried again later. A job that sends, from the server platform's
				test app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_mail_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_mail_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The job returns the send's error as its own, so the jobs plugin tries it again, a little later each
				time.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="No SMTP server: the mailbox under --dev, a refusal anywhere else">
				<p>
					Under <code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts the server) or <code>--debug</code> (as
					tests start it), with no SMTP server, <code>Email.send!</code> answers <code>{"Ok({})"}</code> and
					keeps the message for the dev mailbox, logging <code>email: kept, not sent (no SMTP_URL)</code>.
					Anywhere else the message would be lost, so a server that lists the plugin and has no SMTP server
					exits at start with <code>{"email: SMTP_URL isn't set, so email can't be sent: …"}</code>. Without
					the plugin, each <code>Email.send!</code> answers <code>EmailErr</code> saying the same.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="recipients">Several recipients, Cc, Bcc, Reply-To</Heading>
			<p>
				A message is a record with defaults (<code>Email.Message</code>): only <code>to</code>,
				<code>subject</code> and <code>text</code> are required.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Minutes, to the team"
				source={"Email.send!({\n\tto: \"Ada <ada@example.com>, \\\"Lovelace, Grace\\\" <grace@example.com>\",\n\tcc: [\"lin@example.com\"],\n\tbcc: [\"archive@example.com\"],\n\treply_to: \"team@example.com\",\n\theaders: [(\"List-Unsubscribe\", \"<https://example.com/unsubscribe?t=\${token}>\")],\n\tsubject: \"Minutes\",\n\ttext: minutes,\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>to : Str</code></td><td>(must be given)</td><td>one or several addresses, separated by commas as in an email's <code>To</code> header; a comma inside a quoted name is the name's (<code>{"\"Lovelace, Ada\" <ada@example.com>"}</code>). At least one.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>html : Str</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>the same as HTML; <code>""</code> for text alone</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cc : List(Str)</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>more who see it, in its <code>Cc</code> header; each item one address or several</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>bcc : List(Str)</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>more who get it, named in no header: only the SMTP server is told, so no recipient sees them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>reply_to : Str</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>where a reply goes; <code>""</code>: to the sender</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"headers : List((Str, Str))"}</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>your own headers, such as <code>List-Unsubscribe</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>to</code> stays a <code>Str</code>, so a message for one recipient is unchanged. For a list,
				join it: <code>{"Str.join_with(addresses, \", \")"}</code>. The SMTP server is told each address in
				<code>to</code>, <code>cc</code> and <code>bcc</code> once. If it refuses one, nobody gets the message,
				and <code>Email.send!</code> names the refused address.
			</p>
			<p>
				Your headers can't break the message. One with a line break in its name or value (which would start
				a new header, say a <code>Bcc</code> that quietly adds a recipient) is refused, as are the headers
				the package writes (<code>From</code>, <code>To</code>, <code>Cc</code>, <code>Bcc</code>,
				<code>Reply-To</code>, <code>Subject</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>Message-ID</code>,
				<code>MIME-Version</code>, <code>Content-*</code>): use the field instead. A value that isn't plain
				ASCII is encoded, as the subject is.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="A message you build first is an Email.Message">
				<p>
					A record written straight into <code>Email.send!</code>, or returned by a function typed
					<code>Email.Message</code>, gets the defaults. A function typed with a plain record
					(<code>{"-> { to : Str, subject : Str, text : Str, html : Str }"}</code>) or an untyped local
					doesn't: write <code>Email.Message</code> in its type.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="smtp">Connecting to an SMTP server</Heading>
			<p><code>SMTP_URL</code> (or what your <code>smtp_url!</code> returns) says where to connect and how:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>URL</th><th>Connection</th><th>Default port</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>smtps://user:password@host</code></td><td>TLS from the start</td><td>465</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host?tls=required</code></td><td>STARTTLS; fails if the server doesn't offer it</td><td>587</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host</code></td><td>STARTTLS, required as above: a password never goes in clear</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://host?tls=opportunistic</code></td><td>STARTTLS if the server offers it, plain otherwise (with a user: refused if it isn't offered)</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://host</code></td><td>plain, no TLS (a local relay, or a test server)</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host?tls=never</code></td><td>plain, the password too (a relay on this machine)</td><td>25</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A port in the URL (<code>host:2525</code>) overrides the default. With a user in the URL, the client
				signs in with <code>AUTH PLAIN</code>; write special characters in the user or password as
				<code>%</code> escapes. The password goes in clear only with <code>?tls=never</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Once the server has started, the plugin connects to the SMTP server (TLS and all), says goodbye, and
				logs whether it answered, under <code>email.smtp</code>, so a wrong URL shows in the log, not at the
				first email. An SMTP server that doesn't answer doesn't stop the app.
			</p>

			<Heading id="mailbox">The dev mailbox</Heading>
			<p>
				A server started with <code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts it) answers
				<code>/__rocx/mail</code> before your app. It lists the email kept instead of sent, newest first
				(when, from, to, subject), with a Clear button, updating as mail arrives. Each message has a page at
				<code>{"/__rocx/mail/<id>"}</code>: its headers (Cc, Bcc, Reply-To and your own), its text, its HTML in
				a sandboxed frame (no script runs; links open in a new tab), and the whole message as SMTP would have
				carried it, where no header names the Bcc. The dev server lists the mailbox's address as it starts.
			</p>
			<p>
				The last 100 messages are kept in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}><code>Kv</code></a>),
				with or without a database, so a restart forgets them, on purpose. <code>/__rocx/mail.json</code> has
				them as JSON, for tests: the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_testing}>testing package</a> reads it to find the
				link in a sign-in email. Under <code>--debug</code> without <code>--dev</code>, only the JSON is
				served. Without either flag, these paths are your app's.
			</p>

			<Heading id="inside">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				The package is Roc on what the server platform gives every plugin. <code>Email.roc</code> writes the
				message: it reads every address first, encodes a subject or name that isn't plain ASCII (RFC 2047),
				sends such a body as Base64, and makes text with HTML <code>multipart/alternative</code>.
				<code>Smtp.roc</code> is a small SMTP client over the platform's <code>Tcp</code>, which has TLS: a
				<code>RCPT TO</code> for each recipient, then the message, on a new connection for each send.
				<code>Mailbox.roc</code> is the dev mailbox: paths a plugin answers before the app, only under
				<code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code> (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>),
				drawn in the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>admin's</a> look.
			</p>
			<p>
				Without the plugin, <code>Email.send!</code> still works, reading <code>SMTP_URL</code> and
				<code>MAIL_FROM</code> itself on each send. You lose the mailbox, the SMTP check at start, and the
				refusal to start without a server: a send fails instead.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>No attachments.</li>
				<li>No groups in an address list (<code>{"undisclosed-recipients:;"}</code>), and no comments in an address.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The package's <code>README.md</code> (<code>packages/email/</code>) has the full reference.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
